#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105575 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "d044f208-be6c-508f-b92d-221f36a8edcf" :question/math-academy-id "e-7352" :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The number of heads obtained when a coin is flipped $6$ times\n\n- The number of times a $6$-sided die is rolled until a $6$ appears\n\n- The number of months in a particular year" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: $x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $0,1,2,3,4,5,6.$\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can count its possible values in a sequence: $1,2,3,\\ldots$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a discrete random variable. The number of months in a particular year is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $12$ months in a year.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
